          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have had a cough for two weeks after a cold.",
          "answer": "Based on the reference material I could search, a cough that lasts two to three weeks after a cold is common and is usually not, by itself, a sign of a bacterial infection. The upper respiratory infection summary says cough often outlasts other cold symptoms, and that this is normal. The antibiotic stewardship guidance says the same: a cough alone lasting two to three weeks after a cold is expected.\n\nThe safety check found no emergency pattern in the list it covers. That does not rule out a problem. The influenza summary notes that a deterioration after several days of getting better can be a sign of a secondary bacterial pneumonia, so a worsening pattern is worth raising with a clinician.\n\nYou should contact a doctor or other clinician soon if you notice any of these:\n- The cough is getting worse instead of slowly better, or you feel sicker after an improvement.\n- Fever that is high or returns, or symptoms that keep going past what you expect.\n- Shortness of breath, chest pain, or coughing up blood. If these are severe or sudden, seek emergency care now.\n- Anything new or unexplained that worries you.\n\nThe material I searched did not cover what treatment, if any, would suit your situation, and I cannot tell you whether you need to be seen. A clinician who can examine you is the right person for that.\n\n*Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.*",
